00:02:49.769 --> 00:02:58.090
It feels appropriate since revitalizing the
Star Wars franchise is such a huge deal.

00:02:58.090 --> 00:03:01.360
James Cameron went one step further when he
released the Avatar trailer.

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:04.069
He rented out theaters just so he could show
the trailer.

00:03:04.069 --> 00:03:07.099
It wasn’t attached to films the first time
it premiered.

00:03:07.099 --> 00:03:12.069
Tickets were free but he rented out IMAX theaters
for a special showing before the first trailer

00:03:12.069 --> 00:03:13.069
posted online.

00:03:13.069 --> 00:03:20.810
And if you’re old enough to remember, George
Lucas did the same thing with the Phantom

00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:21.810
Menace trailer.

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:23.189
He showed it before a bunch of movies and
after.

00:03:23.189 --> 00:03:26.269
So you got to see it twice.

00:03:26.269 --> 00:03:30.144
Here’s a really cool article the New York
Times posted about it back in 1998.

00:03:30.144 --> 00:03:31.144
Think about how long ago this was.

00:03:31.144 --> 00:03:32.144
Here’s what they said about it:

00:03:32.144 --> 00:03:33.144
''Star Wars'' is back.

00:03:33.144 --> 00:03:37.760
Or more precisely, a 2 minute 10 second trailer
for ''Star Wars: Episode I -- The Phantom

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:42.110
Menace,'' the first ''Star Wars'' film since
the release of ''Return of the Jedi'' 15 years

00:03:42.110 --> 00:03:43.110
ago.

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:47.730
The new film, written and directed by George
Lucas, opens on May 21, but the anticipation

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:49.650
for it is extraordinary, several distribution
executives say.

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:54.349
The trailer was screened on Tuesday in 26
states on 75 screens selected by the movie's

00:03:54.349 --> 00:03:56.700
distributor, 20th Century Fox.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:00.670
Fans heard about it four days earlier on the
Lucasfilm Web site (www.starwars.com), and

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:04.629
it quickly turned into a film event that had
no precedent.

00:04:04.629 --> 00:04:08.409
Theaters showing films like ''The Waterboy,''
''The Siege'' and ''Meet Joe Black'' were

00:04:08.409 --> 00:04:13.799
crammed with people -- mostly young men -- who
paid full admission just to see the trailer

00:04:13.799 --> 00:04:18.370
and left when the movie began.

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:22.630
The trailer was also shown after each movie
ended, so a handful of theater owners allowed

00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:25.620
filmgoers to return with their torn ticket
stub.

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:35.380
(Historically, trailers used to be shown after
movies ended, hence the name.)

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:41.800
Then here’s a quote from someone at Lucasfilm
at the time talking about the idea for doing

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:42.800
the special screenings

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:44.010
Tom Sherak, the chairman of Fox's Domestic
Film Group, said the idea for the early-in-the-week

00:04:44.010 --> 00:04:46.220
trailer, which was released nationally over
the weekend, came from Mr. Lucas's associates.

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:50.669
''They said since they have such a big fan
base, why not set something up just for the

00:04:50.669 --> 00:04:54.690
fans first and make sure it's shown to the
rest of the public very quickly?'' he said.

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:57.410
''The idea was to figure out a way to have
fun with this.

00:04:57.410 --> 00:05:00.180
'Star Wars' is part of the culture, but it's
still a movie.

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:01.330
Let's treat it like a movie.''

00:05:01.330 --> 00:05:02.410
That time around they only showed it in 30
theaters, so it wasn’t so much a box office

00:05:02.410 --> 00:05:03.410
